    "result_text": "The full suite is green (BUILD SUCCESS).\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented per-country national-number length validation for E.164 telephone numbers in `TelephoneNormalizer.java`.\n\n**What changed:**\n- Added a `NATIONAL_LENGTH_BY_COUNTRY_CODE` map (`\"61\" \u2192 9`, `\"1\" \u2192 10`), ordered longest-prefix-first via `LinkedHashMap` so the most specific country code matches.\n- After the existing generic E.164 format check (8\u201315 digits), `normalize()` now calls a new private helper `requireValidNationalLength(...)` that:\n  - matches the normalized number's country code prefix,\n  - checks that the remaining national digits equal the count that country requires,\n  - throws `InvalidTelephoneException` (which the `ExceptionControllerAdvice` already maps to **400 Bad Request**, naming the `telephone` field) when the length is wrong.\n  - Numbers with an unrecognised country code pass this check (only the generic 8\u201315 rule applies to them).\n\n**Design choices:**\n- The logic lives in the class that already owns E.164 normalization and already parses out the country code \u2014 it's a natural extension of that single responsibility, not a new catch-all. It's isolated in its own small private method with a focused javadoc.\n- Existing exception, advice, and 400 mapping are reused rather than duplicated.\n\nVerified against the provided acceptance case (`+61 123` \u2192 400) and the valid-owner path (AU `04XXXXXXXX` \u2192 `+61` with 9 national digits, and the `+1` NANP seed data with 10 national digits both still accepted). The full test suite passes.",
